00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.969 align:start position:0%
 
is it true that the home office is now  subject to a judicial review over the  treatment of people at the manston  Immigration Center  uh well good evening I believe we have  received the initial contact for a  judicial review That's Not Unusual this  is a highly litigious area of policy but  of course as the minister responsible I  want to ensure that everything that we  do is conducted appropriately and within  the law and so in the week that I've  been in post I have tried to work night  and day to ensure that the manston site  is not just legally compliant but is a  Humane and compassionate place where we  welcome those migrants treat them  appropriately and then they leave  quickly to Alternative accommodation and  I'm very pleased to say this evening  that that's exactly the path that we're  on the number of people at manston has  fallen very substantially since the  weekend when we became aware of the  specific issues and got involved so  directly and I think we're on a path now  whereby within a matter of days assuming  we don't see very large numbers of  further migrants arriving a across the  channel I don't think that's going to  happen because we have good forecasts of  the weather and the other intelligence  that we have from northern France so I  expect that manston will be returned to  a well-functioning and certainly legally  compliant site very rapidly a matter of  days what's changed have you basically  tried to take over Zoella braven's brief  on this nope absolutely not we've been  working extremely closely together and  we've procured more hotels we have extra  support we've got brilliant officers  from border Force supported by  contractors and members of the Armed  Forces and we're processing the migrants  who are in the camp very quickly just on  the judicial review do you know if it's  been brought by I don't think I can  comment on on what might be ongoing  legal action they're not the only people  who are concerned uh this evening four  parliamentary committee chairs including  two conservatives have also written to  ask what the home office is doing to  address the conditions in munston they  worried in particular about the  conditions children are being held in  and the use of thoughts and physical  searches  you know being honest are you worried as  well well when I visited the camp on uh  Sunday I thought conditions were poor  and they needed to change rapidly that's  exactly well mainly because of the  number of people who are there remember  this facility was functioning acceptably  just a matter of weeks ago what's  changed is that we've had a very large  number of individuals across the channel  in small boats we would love to think we  can control that but of course that's  not entirely within our control and then  we had this terrible attack on Sunday by  this individual in Dover which caused us  to evacuate the almost 1 000 people who  are there and bring them immediately to  the site and that increased the numbers  from 3000 which was already too many to  four thousand where there was real  strain on the site I would just say  having been there that some of the  reporting is inaccurate there was  suggestions that there have been  diseases like diphtheria running wild in  the facility that's not correct to the  best of my knowledge there have been  four cases out of thousands of  individuals each of those cases We  Believe came into the site if you like  rather than being caught there there's a  very good medical facility when I was  there on Sunday there were two emergency  doctors there were paramedics and nurses  everybody is clothed they're fed they're  supported whilst they're there just as  you'd expect we you know we have values  as a country just because somebody's  chosen to come as a migrant we don't  want them to come in this way in small  boats but when they're here we'll treat  them appropriately and that's exactly  what we are doing and what we want to  get to uh in the coming days  um it's not just an issue that is about  this one Immigration Center though is it  it's about the strain on the system as a  whole and I just want to look now at a  chart showing the UK Asylum caseload it  effectively shows a rising number of  cases a class is still in process  without a full resolution  I mean is that really good enough no no  it's clearly not good enough and we have  a task to do now as I said I'm new in  the position suela Bravo and the Home  Secretary has only been in position for  six or seven weeks as well so together  we're going to work intensively to  reduce that backlog of cases we've  already put a significant amount of  extra resource behind this we have a  thousand people working on cases the  system is quite slow in this country  compared to some of our comparators like  France and Germany we think we can  improve productivity get people  processing the claims much faster but I  would just say that all of what we've  been talking about in the last week are  the symptoms of the problem the problem  is the fact that 40 000 people have  chosen to get into small boats to cross  the channel at the behest of dangerous  illegal criminal gangs and we are having  to try to cope with that and our  infrastructure was never designed for 40  000 people to come in small boats and to  land on our beaches will be saved at Sea  by border force rnli and the Royal Navy  with the best wool in the world they  would always put immense strain on our  system just to come back because you  said that they've chosen to come  if you're not from Hong Kong or Ukraine  or Afghanistan where there are specific  and bespoke legal ways to claim Asylum  or to claim an immigration claim to come  to the UK  how do you reclaim Asylum legally in the  UK because you only have to make the  claim when you're in the UK and yet  you're supposed to claim in the first  safe country you land in so it's  impossible isn't it no it's not great  when first firstly the numbers on the  schemes you just quoted are very large  so there's a hundred and fifty thousand  people in this country from Ukraine my  point is this is part of the reason why  well if you're from another country and  you feel that you are unsafe and you  want to claim yourself you finish that  point firstly the numbers are very large  from those places so the suggestion made  by some that the UK is not a big-hearted  generous country that's trying to play  its part in a global migration crisis is  not correct because we've issued a  hundred and thirty thousand Hong Kong  bno visas we've got 150 000 people here  under the homes for Ukraine scheme we  bought tens of thousands of Afghans to  the UK in addition to that there are  other safe and legal routes there's a  community sponsorship route which would  enable churches synagogues more  Community groups to sponsor individuals  to come here we have another scheme  where we work with the unhcr to enable  people who are in real distress often in  Refugee campus to say that the advice on  the website says you should apply when  you you should apply for Asylum when you  arrive in the UK and also says your  claim might not be considered if you  travel to the UK through a safe that's  right isn't it well I just I mean I I  think fundamentally that's the right  approach if you're in a safe country  like France that's where you should  claim Asylum I think the British public  are absolutely flabbergasted that there  are people in France who don't deem that  to be a safe country and put themselves  on there often their children as well in  immense danger to come to the UK you  know we want a system where we can  support people who are genuinely in  danger who are escaping War persecution  serious human rights abuses like we're  doing with Hong Kong Afghanistan and  Ukraine we do not want a system that  takes people literally out of the sea  often who are economic migrants and  prioritizes those over people well a  very substantial uh minority perhaps  more are economic migrants I mean if you  look at the example of Albania this year  a quarter of those people who've come in  small boats have come from Albania  Albania is a demonstrably safe country  it's a European country you've got Eddie  Rahman the prime minister of Albania  tonight saying you know don't talk  Albania down we're a safe country it is  not right  targeting Albanian albanians as the  cause of Britain's common border  problems makes the easy rhetoric but  ignores hard fact well I want to have a  constructive and productive relationship  with our Albanian friends that's  extremely that's extremely important but  it is correct that a quarter of people  who've come in small boats have come  from Albany this year and the ncar  National Crime organization has said  that a very significant proportion of  serious organized crime is emanating  from those individuals and again I think  there are millions of people who've been  watching your program this evening who  would think why on Earth is this country  spending thousands of pounds on hotels  on support for young men coming from a  safe country either we shouldn't do that  at all or we should prioritize that on  people in genuine danger such as an  Afghan interpreter and their family who  served the United Kingdom with honor and  distinction and those are the people we  should be working to support now we are  running out of time you're already um  you know close Ally of the Prime  Minister you're described in some  articles as his closest friend in  Parliament it feels like you're doing a  lot of heavy lifting if I may say on the  media rounds such as this evening on one  of the biggest challenging challenges  facing government are you the de facto  Home Secretary no no obviously not and  although though I am good friends with  Rishi sunai my longest friendship in  politics is Sue Ella Brotherman who I've  known for 20 years and I have huge  respect for her she is a great Home  Secretary and one of the things that she  is good at is articulating the genuine  concerns of the British public on this  issue so you haven't been put in  position as immigration Minister I guess  in case the ball did come she and us she  and I are working in lockstep on this  issue because the British public expect  their government to provide safe borders  and a fair and robust immigration system  and that's exactly what we intend to  deliver at warehouse time thank you  thank you

